 "drivingQuestion": "What is the mitzva of ḥaroset according to R' Elazar b. R' Tzadok?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Abaye combines both views in practice",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "pesachim.116a.3",
   "daf": "116a",
   "sentenceId": 2,
   "he": "רַבִּי אֶלְעָזָר בְּרַבִּי צָדוֹק אוֹמֵר מִצְוָה וְכוּ׳. מַאי מִצְוָה? רַבִּי לֵוִי אוֹמֵר: זֵכֶר לַתַּפּוּחַ. וְרַבִּי יוֹחָנָן אוֹמֵר: זֵכֶר לַטִּיט. אָמַר אַבָּיֵי: הִלְכָּךְ צְרִיךְ לְקַהוֹיֵיהּ וּצְרִיךְ לְסַמּוֹכֵיהּ. לְקַהוֹיֵיהּ — זֵכֶר לַתַּפּוּחַ, וּצְרִיךְ לְסַמּוֹכֵיהּ — זֵכֶר לַטִּיט.",
   "en": "The mishna states: «Rabbi Elazar, son of Rabbi Tzadok, says» that eating ḥaroset is «a mitzva.» The Gemara asks: «What» is the nature of this «mitzva?» The Gemara answers: «Rabbi Levi says:» It is «in remembrance of the apple,» as apple is one of the ingredients in ḥaroset. The verse states: “Who is this who comes up from the wilderness, reclining upon her beloved? Under the apple tree I awakened you” (Song of Songs 8:5), which is an allusion to the Jewish people leaving Egypt. «And Rabbi Yoḥanan says:» The ḥaroset is «in remembrance of the mortar» used by the Jews for their slave labor in Egypt. «Abaye said: Therefore,» to fulfill both opinions, one «must» prepare it «tart and» one «must» prepare it «thick.» One must prepare it «tart in remembrance of the apple, and» one «must» prepare it «thick in remembrance of the mortar.»"
  }
 ],
